{"low_number_of_known_fraud_cases": "The low number of known fraud cases refers to the scarcity of documented instances of fraudulent activities, particularly in financial reports and other official documents. This scarcity is highlighted by the imbalance in datasets, where fraudulent cases are vastly outnumbered by non-fraudulent ones."},
{"low_number_of_known_fraud_cases__causes__fraud_detection_to_be_a_challenging_task": "The predicate \"causes\" indicates a direct relationship where the low number of known fraud cases leads to or results in a specific outcome. In this context, it suggests that the scarcity of fraud cases has a consequential impact on the effectiveness or difficulty of a related process."},
{"fraud_detection_to_be_a_challenging_task": "Fraud detection being a challenging task refers to the difficulty in identifying and verifying fraudulent activities within a dataset or system. This challenge is exacerbated by the low number of known fraud cases, which creates an imbalance and makes it harder for detection algorithms or methods to accurately classify and identify fraud."},
