{"lstm": "LSTM, or Long Short-Term Memory, is a type of recurrent neural network (RNN) architecture designed to better capture long-term dependencies and mitigate the vanishing gradient problem often encountered in traditional RNNs.|Here is another description: LSTM, or Long Short-Term Memory, is a type of recurrent neural network (RNN) architecture used in the field of deep learning. Unlike standard feedforward neural networks, LSTM has feedback connections. It is particularly well-suited for modeling sequence data, and in this case, it is chosen over GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) for encoding due to its superior performance on large text sequences.|Here is another description: LSTM, or Long Short-Term Memory, is a type of recurrent neural network (RNN) architecture designed to better capture long-term dependencies and mitigate the vanishing gradient problem often encountered in traditional RNNs. In the context provided, LSTM is utilized as a word encoder within a bidirectional setup to process and annotate text sequences effectively.|Here is another description: LSTM refers to a type of recurrent neural network architecture that is designed to learn long-term dependencies. It is widely used in various applications involving sequence prediction and time series analysis.|Here is another description: LSTM refers to a Long Short-Term Memory network, which is a type of recurrent neural network capable of learning order dependence in sequence prediction problems."},
