{"sec__applies_enforcement_actions_where__the_evidence_of_manipulation_is_sufficiently_robust": "The predicate \"applies enforcement actions where\" indicates the SEC's selective approach in taking action against companies. It signifies that the SEC does not apply enforcement actions indiscriminately but rather focuses on cases where certain criteria are met."},
{"the_evidence_of_manipulation_is_sufficiently_robust": "The object \"the evidence of manipulation is sufficiently robust\" describes the specific criterion the SEC uses to determine when to apply enforcement actions. It means that the SEC will only take action if it has strong and convincing evidence that a company has manipulated its financial reports."},
{"labeling_reports_based_on_the_aaer": "Labeling reports based on the AAER refers to the process of categorizing company reports as fraudulent or non-fraudulent using the SEC's Accounting and Auditing Enforcement Releases. This method is based on whether the company filing the report was convicted of financial reporting violations.|Here is another description: Labeling reports based on the AAER refers to the process of categorizing and marking financial reports as fraudulent or non-fraudulent using the guidelines and information provided by the SEC's Accounting and Auditing Enforcement Releases. This method ensures that the labeling is consistent and replicable."},
{"labeling_reports_based_on_the_aaer__offers__simplicity_and_consistency_with_easy_replication": "The predicate 'offers' in this context signifies that the method of labeling reports using AAER provides certain advantages. It indicates that this approach is beneficial in some way for the task of report categorization."},
