{"binary_fraud_classification": "The object \"binary fraud classification\" describes the method used by the researchers to categorize the annual reports into two categories: fraudulent or non-fraudulent, without distinguishing between truthful and unintentionally misstated reports.|Here is another description: Binary fraud classification is a method used to categorize annual reports into two categories: fraudulent and non-fraudulent. This approach simplifies the process of identifying fraudulent activities in financial reporting."},
{"binary_fraud_classification__implies_that__we_do_not_distinguish_between_truthful_and_unintentionally_misstated_annual_reports": "The phrase \"implies that\" indicates a logical consequence or result of adopting a particular method or approach. In this context, it highlights the outcome of using binary fraud classification for annual reports."},
{"we_do_not_distinguish_between_truthful_and_unintentionally_misstated_annual_reports": "The statement \"we do not distinguish between truthful and unintentionally misstated annual reports\" refers to the limitation of the binary fraud classification approach. It means that this method does not differentiate between reports that are accurate and those that contain errors due to honest mistakes."},
{"the_resulting_data_set": "The resulting data set is a collection of annual reports that have been compiled and filtered based on specific criteria related to financial reporting and fraud detection. It represents a curated selection of reports from a larger pool, focusing on those that are relevant for analysis concerning fraudulent activities."},
{"the_resulting_data_set__contains__187_869_annual_reports_filed_between_1993_and_2019": "The predicate \"contains\" signifies that the resulting data set includes or comprises a certain number of annual reports. This indicates that the data set is not empty but rather populated with a specific quantity of reports that meet the defined criteria."},
{"187_869_annual_reports_filed_between_1993_and_2019": "The object \"187,869 annual reports filed between 1993 and 2019\" refers to the total number of annual reports that are part of the resulting data set. These reports span a significant time period, from 1993 to 2019, and are the subject of analysis for detecting fraudulent activities as per the established criteria."},
{"774_firm_years": "The subject, 774 firm-years, refers to the total number of years across different companies that have been identified as having filed fraudulent reports according to the SEC's AAERs. These reports were filed between 1995 and 2016 and were part of a larger dataset of 187,869 annual reports."},
{"774_firm_years__are_subject_to__enforcement_actions": "The predicate, \"are subject to,\" indicates that the 774 firm-years have been targeted by the SEC for enforcement actions due to the fraudulent nature of their reports. This means that these companies have been found to have violated reporting rules and are now subject to legal consequences."},
{"enforcement_actions": "The object, \"enforcement actions,\" refers to the legal measures taken by the SEC against companies that have been found to have filed fraudulent reports. These actions are detailed in the AAERs and are applied when there is robust evidence of manipulation, ensuring a high degree of trust in the enforcement process."},
{"the_final_data_set": "The final data set refers to the comprehensive collection of annual reports that have been meticulously filtered and processed for analysis. This data set is the result of a rigorous selection process that includes considerations of fraud labeling, binary fraud classification, and the extraction of relevant text and financial data."},
{"the_final_data_set__is_reduced_to__7757_firm_year_observations_with_208_fraud_and_7549_non_fraud_filings": "The phrase \"is reduced to\" indicates a process of refinement or filtering that has been applied to the initial data set. This reduction involves the removal of entries with missing data or mismatches, leading to a more streamlined and analyzable collection of firm-year observations."},
{"7757_firm_year_observations_with_208_fraud_and_7549_non_fraud_filings": "The object \"7757 firm-year observations with 208 fraud and 7549 non-fraud filings\" describes the final, refined data set after the reduction process. This data set includes a total of 7757 observations, categorized into 208 instances of fraudulent filings and 7549 instances of non-fraudulent filings, ready for further analysis."},
{"we__perform__the_extraction_of_text_and_financial_data": "The predicate \"perform\" indicates the action taken by the subject. In this context, it means carrying out or executing a specific task, which in this case is the extraction of data from the company reports."},
{"the_extraction_of_text_and_financial_data": "The object \"the extraction of text and financial data\" refers to the specific task performed by the subject. This involves retrieving and processing both textual information and financial figures from the annual reports to aid in the analysis and classification of fraudulent activities."},
{"md_a_section__constitutes__primary_source_of_raw_text_data": "The term 'constitutes' indicates that the MD&A section is recognized as forming or making up the primary source of raw text data in the study. This means that the data extracted from the MD&A section is fundamental and essential for the research being conducted."},
{"primary_source_of_raw_text_data": "The primary source of raw text data refers to the main and original data set used in the study, which in this case is derived from the MD&A section of financial reports. This data is crucial for the analysis and is the foundation upon which the study's conclusions are based."},
{"nine_linguistic_features": "Nine linguistic features refer to a set of specific language characteristics identified and selected for analysis in the study. These features are chosen based on previous research indicating patterns associated with fraudulent behavior, such as the use of negative words, lack of certainty, and heightened sentiment."},
{"nine_linguistic_features__are_utilized_as__predictors": "The phrase \"are utilized as\" indicates that the nine linguistic features are being employed or applied in a specific role or function within the study. In this context, it means that these features are being used to serve a particular purpose or to achieve a specific outcome in the research."},
{"predictors": "Predictors in this context refer to variables or factors that are used to forecast or indicate the likelihood of a particular outcome. In the study, the nine linguistic features are employed as predictors to help identify patterns or signals that may be associated with fraudulent behavior based on the textual data analyzed."},
{"selection_of_features": "The selection of features refers to the process of choosing specific characteristics or attributes from a dataset to be used in a study or analysis. In this context, it involves picking nine linguistic features that serve as predictors for the research."},
{"selection_of_features__is_influenced_by__past_studies": "The phrase \"is influenced by\" indicates that the selection process of the features is not arbitrary but rather guided and shaped by external factors or previous research. It suggests a dependency or reliance on established knowledge or findings to inform the current choices."},
{"past_studies": "Past studies refer to previous research or investigations that have been conducted in the relevant field. These studies provide a foundation of knowledge and insights that can inform and guide current research practices, including the selection of features for analysis.|Here is another description: Past studies refer to previous research efforts and investigations that have been conducted to understand various phenomena or to test hypotheses. In the context of this study, past studies are those that have explored the linguistic patterns and behaviors associated with fraudulent activities.|Here is another description: Past studies refer to previous research efforts and academic investigations that have been conducted in a particular field of study. In this context, past studies are those that have explored the use of financial variables in capturing financial distress and managerial motivations to misrepresent firm performance."},
{"past_studies__demonstrated__patterns_of_fraudulent_agents": "The predicate \"demonstrated\" indicates that the past studies provided evidence or showed clear examples of certain phenomena. In this context, it means that the previous research has successfully shown or proven the existence of specific patterns associated with fraudulent agents."},
{"patterns_of_fraudulent_agents": "Patterns of fraudulent agents refer to the consistent behaviors, linguistic styles, or characteristics that are commonly observed in individuals or entities engaged in fraudulent activities. These patterns may include the use of certain types of language, sentiment, or other linguistic features that distinguish fraudulent communications from honest ones."},
{"fraudulent_agents": "Fraudulent agents refer to individuals or entities involved in deceptive or dishonest activities, particularly in financial or business contexts. In this study, they are the focus of analysis to identify specific linguistic patterns that may indicate fraudulent behavior."},
{"fraudulent_agents__have__increased_likelihood_of_using_words_that_indicate_negativity": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or exhibition of a particular characteristic or behavior by fraudulent agents. It establishes a relationship between the subject (fraudulent agents) and the object (increased likelihood of using words that indicate negativity)."},
{"increased_likelihood_of_using_words_that_indicate_negativity": "The increased likelihood of using words that indicate negativity refers to a pattern observed in the language used by fraudulent agents. This pattern involves a higher frequency of negative words, which may be a telltale sign of their deceptive intentions or actions."},
{"absence_of_process_ownership": "The absence of process ownership refers to a situation where there is no clear accountability or responsibility assigned to specific individuals or teams for particular processes within an organization. This lack of ownership can lead to confusion and inefficiency in managing and executing tasks."},
{"absence_of_process_ownership__implies__lack_of_assurance": "Implies suggests a logical or causal relationship where the absence of process ownership leads to or results in a lack of assurance. This means that when there is no clear ownership of processes, it is likely that there will be a consequent lack of confidence or certainty in the outcomes or results of those processes."},
{"lack_of_assurance": "Lack of assurance indicates a state where there is insufficient confidence or certainty about the reliability, accuracy, or effectiveness of processes or outcomes within an organization. This can result in doubts about the integrity or validity of the data, reports, or actions taken, potentially leading to further issues such as reduced trust and increased risk of errors or fraud.|Here is another description: Lack of assurance refers to a situation where there is no confidence or certainty in the information being presented, often due to the absence of process ownership or other factors."},
{"lack_of_assurance__results_in__statements_containing_less_certainty": "Results in indicates a cause-and-effect relationship where the lack of assurance leads to a specific outcome, in this case, affecting the nature of statements made."},
{"statements_containing_less_certainty": "Statements containing less certainty are expressions or declarations that lack strong conviction or definitiveness, often reflecting the underlying lack of assurance in the subject matter."},
{"fraudulent_reports": "Fraudulent reports refer to documents or statements that contain false or misleading information, often used to deceive stakeholders or manipulate financial outcomes.|Here is another description: Fraudulent reports refer to documents or statements that contain false or misleading information, often used to deceive stakeholders or manipulate financial outcomes. In the context of this study, fraudulent reports are analyzed for their linguistic features to distinguish them from honest reports."},
{"fraudulent_reports__have__average_of_three_times_more_positive_sentiment": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or characteristic of containing a certain attribute, specifically an average sentiment level that is notably different from honest reports."},
{"average_of_three_times_more_positive_sentiment": "An average of three times more positive sentiment indicates that fraudulent reports tend to use significantly more positive language compared to honest reports, which is a distinctive linguistic feature identified in the study."},
{"fraudulent_reports__have__four_times_more_negative_sentiment_in_comparison_to_honest_reports": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or characteristic of a certain attribute. Here, it indicates that fraudulent reports possess a specific quality\u2014a higher level of negative sentiment\u2014when compared to honest reports."},
{"four_times_more_negative_sentiment_in_comparison_to_honest_reports": "Four times more negative sentiment in comparison to honest reports indicates a significant difference in the emotional tone of fraudulent reports. This means that fraudulent reports contain a much higher frequency of words and phrases that convey negative emotions, such as dissatisfaction, anger, or disappointment, than honest reports."},
{"hajek_and_henriques__included__general_tone_sentiment_and_the_proportion_of_constraining_words": "The term 'included' in this context signifies that Hajek and Henriques incorporated or added certain elements into their analysis or study. This action indicates their methodological approach to evaluating textual content in financial reports."},
{"general_tone_sentiment_and_the_proportion_of_constraining_words": "The 'general tone (sentiment) and the proportion of constraining words' refers to specific metrics used by Hajek and Henriques in their analysis. The general tone assesses the overall emotional or attitudinal sentiment of the text, while the proportion of constraining words measures the use of words that limit or qualify statements, providing insights into the cautiousness or assertiveness of the language used.|Here is another description: The 'general tone (sentiment)' refers to the overall emotional or attitudinal quality conveyed by the text, which can be positive, negative, or neutral. The 'proportion of constraining words' indicates the frequency of words that impose limitations or conditions within the text, which can influence the interpretation and implications of the content.|Here is another description: The \"general tone (sentiment) and the proportion of constraining words\" refers to specific textual analysis metrics. The general tone or sentiment likely measures the overall positive or negative sentiment of a text, while the proportion of constraining words could refer to words that limit or qualify statements, possibly indicating uncertainty or conditions."},
{"loughran_and_mcdonald__included__general_tone_sentiment_and_the_proportion_of_constraining_words": "The term 'included' signifies that Loughran and Mcdonald incorporated specific elements into their analysis or study. This action implies a deliberate choice to consider these elements as part of their research methodology."},
{"bodnaruk_et_al": "Bodnaruk et al. is a group of researchers or authors who have contributed to a study or analysis, likely in the field of financial or textual analysis given the context."},
{"bodnaruk_et_al__included__general_tone_sentiment_and_the_proportion_of_constraining_words": "The term \"included\" in this context signifies that Bodnaruk et al. incorporated or added certain elements into their study or analysis. It indicates that these elements were considered relevant and were part of the methodology or framework used by the authors."},
{"average_length_of_sentence": "The average length of sentence is a metric used to evaluate the complexity and readability of written text. It is calculated by dividing the total number of words in a text by the number of sentences."},
{"average_length_of_sentence__incorporated_as__measures_of_complexity_and_legibility": "Incorporated as refers to the inclusion of a specific metric or measure within a broader set of criteria or analysis. In this context, it signifies that the average length of sentence is being used as one of several measures to assess complexity and legibility."},
{"measures_of_complexity_and_legibility": "Measures of complexity and legibility are criteria used to evaluate how difficult or easy it is to understand and read a text. These measures can include various metrics such as sentence length, word complexity, and the use of constraining words, among others.|Here is another description: Measures of complexity and legibility are criteria used to assess how difficult a text is to understand and how easily it can be read. These measures help in evaluating the overall readability and comprehensibility of a document.|Here is another description: Measures of complexity and legibility refer to various metrics and indices used to evaluate how difficult or easy a text is to understand and read, which can include factors like sentence length, word complexity, and overall text structure."},
{"proportion_of_compound_words": "The proportion of compound words refers to the percentage of words in a text that are made up of two or more simpler words joined together. This metric is often used to assess the complexity of the language used in a document."},
{"proportion_of_compound_words__incorporated_as__measures_of_complexity_and_legibility": "Incorporated as signifies that the proportion of compound words is included or integrated into a set of criteria or measures. In this context, it is used to evaluate certain attributes of a text."},
{"fog_index": "The fog index is a metric used to assess the readability of text, specifically by measuring the complexity of the language used."},
{"fog_index__incorporated_as__measures_of_complexity_and_legibility": "The phrase 'incorporated as' indicates that the fog index is included or adopted as part of a set of criteria or measures."},
{"text_features": "Text features refer to the characteristics or attributes extracted from textual data, which are used in conjunction with other types of data for analysis. In this context, text features are utilized alongside quantitative financial predictors to enhance the understanding and prediction of financial distress and managerial motivations."},
{"text_features__used_along_with__47_quantitative_financial_predictors": "The phrase \"used along with\" indicates a complementary relationship between text features and 47 quantitative financial predictors. This means that both sets of data are employed together to provide a more comprehensive analysis, leveraging the strengths of each to improve the accuracy and robustness of the predictions."},
{"47_quantitative_financial_predictors": "The 47 quantitative financial predictors are a set of numerical indicators derived from financial data, designed to capture various aspects of financial distress and managerial motivations. These predictors include financial ratios, balance sheet variables, and other financial metrics extracted from Compustat, following established formulas and guidelines from prior research.|Here is another description: The subject refers to a set of 47 quantitative financial predictors used in a study to capture financial distress and managerial motivations to misrepresent firm performance. These predictors are based on financial ratios and balance sheet variables.|Here is another description: The 47 quantitative financial predictors are a set of financial ratios and balance sheet variables used to assess various aspects of a firm's financial health. These predictors are detailed in an online appendix and are sourced from Compustat, following established formulas and guidelines from prior research.|Here is another description: The 47 quantitative financial predictors are a set of financial metrics and ratios used to analyze and predict various aspects of a firm's financial health and performance. These predictors are detailed in an online appendix and are sourced from Compustat, following established formulas and guidelines from past research."},
{"47_quantitative_financial_predictors__described_in__the_online_appendix": "The predicate indicates that these 47 quantitative financial predictors are detailed or explained within a specific document, in this case, the online appendix of the study."},
{"the_online_appendix": "The object is the online appendix, which is a supplementary document attached to the main study. It provides additional information, including the detailed descriptions and formulas for the 47 quantitative financial predictors used in the research.|Here is another description: The Online Appendix is a supplementary section of a document, typically found at the end, that contains additional information, data, or examples that support the main content of the document."},
{"47_quantitative_financial_predictors__capable_of__capturing_financial_distress": "The capability of capturing financial distress refers to the effectiveness of the 47 quantitative financial predictors in identifying signs of financial instability within a firm. This involves detecting potential issues such as declining profitability, increased noncash working capital, and other indicators that may signal impending financial trouble."},
{"capturing_financial_distress": "Capturing financial distress involves the identification and assessment of various indicators that suggest a firm is experiencing financial instability. This can include declining profitability ratios, increased noncash working capital, and other financial metrics that signal potential fraud or mismanagement, as supported by theoretical evidence and past studies."},
{"47_quantitative_financial_predictors__capable_of__capturing_managerial_motivations_to_misrepresent_the_performance_of_the_firm": "The phrase \"capable of\" indicates that the 47 quantitative financial predictors have the ability or potential to achieve a specific outcome. In this context, it means that these predictors are effective in identifying and measuring certain financial behaviors or conditions within a firm."},
{"capturing_managerial_motivations_to_misrepresent_the_performance_of_the_firm": "Managerial motivations to misrepresent the performance of the firm refer to the incentives or reasons that managers may have to present a false or misleading picture of the company\u2019s financial health. This can include inflating earnings, hiding losses, or manipulating financial statements to meet certain targets or expectations."},
{"past_studies__presented__robust_theoretical_evidence_supporting_the_utilization_of_financial_variables": "The predicate \"presented\" indicates that the past studies have put forward or showcased certain findings, arguments, or evidence. In this context, it means that the past studies have demonstrated or provided robust theoretical evidence."},
{"robust_theoretical_evidence_supporting_the_utilization_of_financial_variables": "Robust theoretical evidence supporting the utilization of financial variables refers to strong, well-founded, and reliable theoretical arguments or proofs that advocate for the use of financial variables. In this context, it signifies the solid theoretical backing that past studies have provided for employing financial variables to capture financial distress and managerial motivations to misrepresent firm performance."},
{"financial_ratios_and_balance_sheet_variables": "Financial ratios and balance sheet variables are quantitative measures used to evaluate a company's financial health and performance. These include metrics like profitability ratios, total assets, accounts receivable, and inventories, which serve as indicators of financial distress and managerial motivations.|Here is another description: Financial ratios and balance sheet variables are quantitative metrics derived from a company's financial statements, used to assess its financial health, performance, and potential risks. These variables include indicators like total assets, profitability ratios, and noncash working capital drivers."},
{"financial_ratios_and_balance_sheet_variables__extracted_from__compustat": "The predicate \"extracted from\" indicates the source or origin of the financial ratios and balance sheet variables. In this context, it specifies that these financial metrics are derived or obtained from a specific database or resource."},
{"compustat": "Compustat is a comprehensive financial database that provides historical data on global companies. It is widely used in academic research and financial analysis to obtain quantitative financial information, including ratios and balance sheet variables, which are essential for evaluating company performance and financial health."},
{"financial_ratios_and_balance_sheet_variables__based_on_formulas_presented_by__dechow_et_al_15_and_beneish_6": "The phrase \"based on formulas presented by\" indicates that the financial ratios and balance sheet variables are calculated using specific mathematical formulas. These formulas have been established and documented by recognized researchers in the field."},
{"dechow_et_al_15_and_beneish_6": "Dechow et al. [15] and Beneish [6] are researchers who have contributed significantly to the field of financial analysis by developing and presenting formulas for calculating various financial ratios and balance sheet variables. Their work provides a theoretical and practical foundation for assessing financial distress and detecting potential misrepresentation of firm performance."},
{"financial_variables__include__indicators_like_total_assets_adopted_as_a_proxy_for_company_size_4_68_profitability_ratios_26_accounts_receivable_and_inventories_as_noncash_working_capital_drivers_1_12_55": "The predicate \"include\" signifies that the subject encompasses or consists of the specified elements, indicating a comprehensive list of components that fall under the category of financial variables."},
{"indicators_like_total_assets_adopted_as_a_proxy_for_company_size_4_68_profitability_ratios_26_accounts_receivable_and_inventories_as_noncash_working_capital_drivers_1_12_55": "Indicators like total assets (adopted as a proxy for company size), profitability ratios, and accounts receivable and inventories as noncash working capital drivers are specific metrics used within financial analysis to evaluate different dimensions of a company's financial status and operational efficiency."},
{"a_reduced_ratio_of_sales_general_and_administrative_expenses_sga_to_revenues_sgai": "A reduced ratio of sales general and administrative expenses (SGA) to revenues (SGAI) refers to a situation where the proportion of SGA expenses relative to the company's revenues has decreased. This ratio is an important financial indicator used to assess the efficiency of a company's operations and its ability to manage overhead costs."},
{"a_reduced_ratio_of_sales_general_and_administrative_expenses_sga_to_revenues_sgai__found_to__signalize_fraud_1": "The predicate \"found to\" indicates that empirical research or analysis has established a connection or correlation between the subject and the object. In this context, it means that studies have discovered a link between a reduced SGAI ratio and the occurrence of fraud."},
{"signalize_fraud_1": "Signalizing fraud in this context means that a reduced SGAI ratio serves as an indicator or warning sign that fraudulent activities may be taking place within a company. Fraud can encompass a range of deceptive practices aimed at misrepresenting a firm's financial performance or condition."},
{"missing_values": "Missing values refer to the absence of data points in a dataset, which can occur for various reasons such as data entry errors, incomplete records, or data corruption. In the context of financial analysis, missing values can significantly impact the accuracy and reliability of predictive models."},
{"missing_values__imputed_using__the_rf_algorithm": "Imputed using signifies the process of filling in missing data points within a dataset using a specific method or algorithm. This process is crucial in data analysis to ensure that the dataset is complete and can be effectively utilized in predictive modeling or statistical analysis."},
{"the_rf_algorithm": "The RF algorithm, or Random Forest algorithm, is a machine learning technique used for both classification and regression tasks. It operates by constructing multiple decision trees during training and outputting the class that is the mode of the classes (classification) or mean prediction (regression) of the individual trees. In the context of imputing missing values, the RF algorithm is employed to predict and fill in the missing data points based on the patterns and relationships observed in the existing data."},
{"previous_research": "Previous research refers to the studies and investigations conducted prior to the current study. These studies have focused on various aspects of fraud detection and analysis, contributing to the existing body of knowledge in the field."},
{"previous_research__has_balanced__fraud_and_nonfraud_cases": "The predicate \"has balanced\" indicates that previous research has taken steps to ensure an equal representation of fraud and nonfraud cases in their datasets. This balancing is crucial for creating a fair and unbiased analysis, as it prevents any one category from dominating the results."},
{"fraud_and_nonfraud_cases": "Fraud and nonfraud cases represent the two categories of data used in previous research. Fraud cases involve instances where fraudulent behavior has occurred, while nonfraud cases involve instances where no fraudulent behavior has been detected. Balancing these cases is important for maintaining the integrity of the research and ensuring that the results are not skewed by an overrepresentation of one category."},
{"data_set__using__undersampling": "The method of balancing the data set involves using a specific technique to adjust the proportions of fraud and non-fraud cases, ensuring that the resulting data set more accurately represents the typical fraud-to-non-fraud ratio observed in practice."},
{"undersampling": "Undersampling is a technique used to balance datasets by reducing the number of instances in the majority class (non-fraud cases in this context) to match the number of instances in the minority class (fraud cases), thereby creating a more balanced dataset for analysis."},
{"we__follow__this_approach": "Follow indicates that the authors are adopting or adhering to a particular approach or methodology that has been used in previous research."},
{"this_approach": "This approach refers to the method of balancing fraud and non-fraud cases in a dataset using undersampling, with a specific fraud-to-non-fraud ratio of 1:4, and considering both year and sector for balancing."},
{"fraud_to_non_fraud_ratio": "The fraud-to-non-fraud-ratio is a metric used to describe the proportion of fraudulent cases to non-fraudulent cases within a dataset. It is employed in research to balance datasets and reflect the prevalence of fraudulent behavior among firms."},
{"fraud_to_non_fraud_ratio__is__1_4": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the specific ratio that has been set for the fraud-to-non-fraud cases in the dataset. It indicates the relationship between the number of fraud cases and non-fraud cases."},
{"1_4": "The object \"1:4\" represents the ratio of fraud to non-fraud cases in the dataset. This ratio suggests that for every one case of fraud, there are four cases of non-fraud, reflecting the reality that most firms do not engage in fraudulent activities."},
{"1_4_ratio": "The 1:4 ratio refers to the proportion of fraud to non-fraud cases in a dataset, which is a common approach in previous research to balance the data."},
{"1_4_ratio__reflects__majority_of_firms_have_no_involvement_in_fraudulent_behaviour": "The predicate \"reflects\" indicates that the 1:4 ratio is representative of a certain reality or condition, in this case, the behavior of firms regarding fraudulent activities."},
{"majority_of_firms_have_no_involvement_in_fraudulent_behaviour": "The object \"majority of firms have no involvement in fraudulent behaviour\" describes the real-world condition that most companies do not engage in fraudulent activities, which is mirrored by the 1:4 ratio in the dataset."},
{"both_year_and_sector": "Both year and sector refer to the temporal and industry-specific dimensions considered in the balancing process of fraud and nonfraud cases in a dataset."},
{"both_year_and_sector__are_utilized_for__balancing": "Are utilized for indicates that both year and sector are employed as criteria or factors in the process of balancing fraud and nonfraud cases, to ensure a more accurate and representative dataset."},
{"balancing": "Balancing in this context refers to the process of equalizing the proportion of fraud and nonfraud cases in a dataset, typically through undersampling, to better reflect the reality that most firms are not involved in fraudulent behavior.|Here is another description: Balancing in the context of research on fraud and nonfraud cases involves adjusting the data set to reflect a more realistic ratio of fraudulent to non-fraudulent behavior, typically using methods like undersampling.|Here is another description: Balancing in the context of research refers to the process of equalizing the representation of different categories within a dataset, such as fraud and non-fraud cases, to ensure a more accurate analysis. This technique is crucial for maintaining the integrity of the data and for drawing reliable conclusions from the study.|Here is another description: Balancing in this context refers to the process of adjusting the proportions of fraud and non-fraud cases within a dataset to ensure a more equitable representation, which is crucial for accurate analysis and modeling."},
{"balancing__takes_into_account__different_economic_conditions": "Taking into account different economic conditions means considering the varying financial environments and economic factors that can influence the occurrence of fraud, ensuring that the balanced data set is representative of real-world scenarios."},
