{"ribeiro_et_al": "Ribeiro et al. refers to a group of researchers, led by Marco Tulio Ribeiro, who are known for their contributions to the field of interpretable machine learning, including the development of the _lime_ methodology.|Here is another description: Ribeiro et al. refers to the authors of a study or method that utilizes the LIME (Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations) technique to interpret machine learning models' decisions. Their work focuses on making the predictions of complex models more transparent and understandable."},
{"lime": "Lime is a method used to understand the role of different words in a model's classification decision. It is applied to gain insights into how specific words influence the model's determination, particularly in identifying fraudulent activities.|Here is another description: Lime, short for Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations, is a method used to gain insight into the role of different words in a model's classification decision by explaining how the model functions locally around a specific observation."},
{"lime__stands_for__local_interpretable_model_agnostic_explanations": "\"Stands for\" indicates that Lime is an abbreviation or shorthand for a more detailed term. In this context, it clarifies that Lime is not just a random name but represents a specific concept or methodology."},
{"local_interpretable_model_agnostic_explanations": "Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations is the full term that Lime stands for. It refers to a technique designed to explain the predictions of any machine learning model by focusing on the local behavior around specific observations, making it easier to understand and interpret the model's decisions."},
{"lime__is_based_on__explaining_the_model_functioning_in_the_locality_of_the_chosen_observation": "The phrase 'is based on' indicates that Lime's functionality and approach are fundamentally rooted in the concept of explaining the model's behavior within the local context of a chosen observation."},
{"explaining_the_model_functioning_in_the_locality_of_the_chosen_observation": "Explaining the model functioning in the locality of the chosen observation refers to the process of interpreting how a model makes decisions by focusing on the specific area or context around a particular data point, providing clarity on the model's decision-making process in that localized setting."},
{"ribeiro_et_al__explains__every_input_separately": "The predicate \"explains\" in this context signifies the action of providing clarity or interpretation. Specifically, it denotes the process by which Ribeiro et al.'s method elucidates the factors or features that influence a model's classification decision, making the model's inner workings more comprehensible."},
{"every_input_separately": "\"Every input separately\" refers to the approach taken by Ribeiro et al.'s method to analyze and interpret each individual input or data point independently. This means that the method assesses the contribution of each input feature to the model's decision on a case-by-case basis, rather than applying a generalized explanation across all inputs."},
{"lime_package": "The Lime package, also known as Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations, is a tool used to explain the functioning of a model in the locality of a chosen observation. It is applied to gain insights into the role of different words in the model's classification decision.|Here is another description: The Lime package is a tool used for providing local interpretable model-agnostic explanations. It helps in understanding the decisions made by complex models by explaining the model's behavior in the context of specific observations."},
{"lime_package__supplies__all_fraud_cases": "The predicate \"supplies\" indicates that the Lime package provides or furnishes something necessary for a process or function. In this context, it refers to the Lime package providing all fraud cases for analysis."},
{"all_fraud_cases": "All fraud cases refer to the complete set of instances or examples of fraudulent activity that are being analyzed. These cases are supplied through the Lime package to extract important words that have the strongest effect on the model in terms of fraud indication."},
