{"rf__managed_to_extract__knowledge_from_both_financial_and_bow_based_textual_sources": "The phrase'managed to extract' indicates RF's capability to effectively utilize and process data from diverse sources. This extraction involves analyzing and interpreting the data to derive meaningful insights or patterns, which are crucial for making accurate predictions."},
{"knowledge_from_both_financial_and_bow_based_textual_sources": "The knowledge extracted by RF comes from two distinct sources: financial data and BOW (Bag of Words)-based textual sources. Financial data typically includes numerical information related to financial transactions, ratios, and other economic indicators. BOW-based textual sources refer to text data processed using the Bag of Words model, which represents text by the occurrence of words, disregarding grammar and word order but keeping the multiplicity of words."},
{"dl_models__proved_capable_of__distinguishing_fraudulent_cases": "The phrase \"proved capable of\" indicates that the DL models have demonstrated their effectiveness or ability in a specific task. In this case, the models have shown that they can successfully perform the task of identifying or differentiating between fraudulent and non-fraudulent cases based on the data they have been trained on."},
{"distinguishing_fraudulent_cases": "Distinguishing fraudulent cases refers to the ability of the DL models to accurately identify instances of fraud within a dataset. This involves recognizing specific patterns, anomalies, or \"red-flag\" indicators that are characteristic of fraudulent behavior, thereby aiding in the detection and prevention of fraud."},
{"han_architecture__showcased__exceptional_capacity_to_extract_signals_from_the_fin_txt_setting": "The term \"showcased\" indicates that the HAN architecture demonstrated or exhibited a particular capability or performance in a specific context, which in this case is its ability to extract meaningful signals from combined financial and textual data."},
{"exceptional_capacity_to_extract_signals_from_the_fin_txt_setting": "The exceptional capacity to extract signals from the FIN + TXT setting refers to the HAN architecture's superior ability to identify and utilize relevant information from both financial data (FIN) and textual data (TXT) to enhance fraud detection capabilities."},
{"han__detects__a_high_number_of_fraudulent_cases_compared_to_remaining_models": "The predicate \"detects\" refers to the capability of the HAN model to identify and flag instances of fraud within a dataset. This involves analyzing both financial and textual data to discern patterns indicative of fraudulent activity."},
{"a_high_number_of_fraudulent_cases_compared_to_remaining_models": "A high number of fraudulent cases compared to remaining models indicates that the HAN model outperforms other models in identifying instances of fraud. This suggests that HAN is more effective at detecting fraud, possibly due to its ability to leverage contextual information from text."},
{"zhou_et_al__stated__the_detection_of_deception_based_on_text_necessitates_contextual_information": "The predicate \"stated\" indicates that Zhou et al. have explicitly mentioned or asserted a particular viewpoint or finding in their research. In this context, it highlights their assertion about the importance of contextual information in detecting deception through text."},
{"the_detection_of_deception_based_on_text_necessitates_contextual_information": "The detection of deception based on text necessitates contextual information refers to the idea that understanding and identifying deceptive content in textual data requires more than just the text itself; it needs the context in which the text is used, such as the surrounding circumstances, financial data, or other relevant information, to accurately determine if deception is present."},
{"auc_measures": "AUC measures are statistical metrics used to evaluate the performance of a classification model, particularly in distinguishing between different classes. In the context of fraud detection, AUC (Area Under the Curve) measures help assess how well a model can differentiate between fraudulent and non-fraudulent cases."},
{"auc_measures__indicate__that_linguistic_variables_extracted_with_han_and_tf_idf_add_significant_value_to_fraud_detection_models_in_combination_with_financial_ratios": "The predicate \"indicate\" in this context signifies that the AUC measures provide evidence or suggest a particular outcome or conclusion. Here, it shows the effectiveness or added value of certain methods or variables in enhancing the performance of fraud detection models."},
{"that_linguistic_variables_extracted_with_han_and_tf_idf_add_significant_value_to_fraud_detection_models_in_combination_with_financial_ratios": "The object describes the finding that linguistic variables, when extracted using HAN (Hierarchical Attention Network) and TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency), significantly enhance the performance of fraud detection models when combined with financial ratios. This suggests that incorporating textual information alongside financial data improves the model's ability to detect fraud."},
{"heterogeneity_in_performance": "The heterogeneity in performance refers to the varying effectiveness and outcomes observed across different models when applied to fraud detection tasks. This variability is crucial as it highlights the diverse capabilities of models in capturing and interpreting signals from various data sources."},
{"heterogeneity_in_performance__shifts_among__different_data_types_for_models": "The phrase \"shifts among\" indicates a dynamic and changing relationship where the performance of models is not static but varies depending on the data types used. This predicate underscores the fluid nature of model effectiveness in different contexts."},
{"different_data_types_for_models": "Different data types for models refer to the varied sources of information that models can utilize, such as financial data, textual data, or a combination of both. These data types influence how well a model can perform in detecting fraud, showcasing the importance of selecting appropriate data for optimal model performance."},
{"different_models": "Different models refer to various machine learning algorithms or architectures used in the study, such as SVM, RF, and DL models like HAN, which are applied to detect fraudulent cases using financial and textual data."},
